<div style="border: 2px solid #1a5276; padding: 15px; text-align: center; background-color: #eaf2f8;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 4</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; margin: 5px 0 0 0;"><em>Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</em></p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 4</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Writing</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 2nd Grade (CEFR: Pre-A1.4)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Discovering Nature’s Wonders</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> It's the Rainforest.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Writing: Productive: Make a list of items. Interactive: Write words or phrases as responses to classroom polls or questions.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Writing: Can make a list of items related to nature or animals (e.g., "tree, bird, rock"). Can write words or phrases as responses to classroom polls, questions, or cues about animals or nature (e.g., Cue: "slow animal." Response: "sloth." Cue: "colorful bird." Response: "parrot." Poll: "Which animal do you like?" Response: "monkey."). Can write simple phrases or sentences about animals or their environments with guidance (e.g., "The sloth lives in trees.").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333; background-color: #f2f2f2;">
        <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%;">Stage & Description</th>
        <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
        <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 34%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
    </tr>

    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling, and Clarification to activate prior knowledge and introduce the writing focus.</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher greets students, plays a short rainforest sounds audio, and displays flashcards of rainforest animals (sloth, toucan, monkey, parrot, frog, snake). Teacher asks, "What animal is this?" and students respond chorally. Teacher uses TPR to act out some animals (e.g., "The monkey swings!").</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher holds up a flashcard, e.g., "sloth," and says "This is a sloth. It is slow and furry." The teacher then writes the word "sloth" clearly on the board. Next, the teacher points to a "colorful parrot" flashcard, says "This is a colorful parrot," and writes "colorful parrot" on the board, demonstrating writing of adjectives + nouns. Teacher emphasizes the initial sound of 's' in 'sloth' and 'r' in 'rainforest' while writing.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Pointing to 'sloth' on the board) "Is this a fast animal or a slow animal?" (Expected: "Slow animal.")</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Pointing to 'parrot') "Is the parrot green or colorful?" (Expected: "Colorful.")</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What am I doing now? Am I drawing or writing?" (Expected: "Writing!")</li>
            </ul>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 minutes</p>
        </td>
    </tr>

    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to the scenario "Discovering Nature’s Wonders," focusing on visuals and simple written forms.</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Large, illustrated poster or picture book page featuring a rainforest scene with animals (sloth, toucan, monkey, frog, jaguar, leaf, river, waterfall, vines) and simple, short descriptive sentences next to each. Examples: "This is a leafy tree." "A slow sloth lives here." "It's a colorful toucan." "A jumpy frog." "A wild jaguar."</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Identifying basic information in short, illustrated descriptions to prepare for writing.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher points to each animal/item on the poster and reads the corresponding sentence aloud, tracing the words with a finger. Students repeat chorally.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher asks simple gist questions: "What place is this?" (Expected: "Rainforest!") "Do you see a monkey?" (Expected: "Yes!")</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher points to a 'sloth' on the poster and asks, "What animal is it?" (Expected: "Sloth!") Then asks, "Is it fast or slow?" (Expected: "Slow!"). Teacher briefly writes "slow sloth" again on the board.</li>
            </ul>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 minutes</p>
        </td>
    </tr>

    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Writing, incorporating target grammar and vocabulary.</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Writing words, phrases, and simple sentences about rainforest animals and their environments. Focus on Adjectives + nouns (e.g., slow sloth, colorful parrot), Present simple ("It's the rainforest," "The sloth lives in trees"), and key vocabulary (sloth, toucan, monkey, parrot, frog, leaf, river, tree, live, slow, furry, colorful, jumpy).</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 1 (Controlled Practice - Word Matching & Copying):</b> Students receive a worksheet with pictures of rainforest animals (e.g., sloth, monkey, toucan, frog) and a word bank below. Students draw a line from each picture to its correct name in the word bank. Then, they copy each word next to the corresponding picture. Teacher circulates, offering help and monitoring handwriting.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 2 (Semi-controlled - Sentence Completion):</b> Students receive a worksheet with incomplete simple sentences and corresponding pictures or word prompts. Examples:
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"The ___ is slow." (Picture of a sloth) -> Students write "sloth."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"It's a colorful ___." (Picture of a parrot) -> Students write "parrot."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"A ___ frog." (Picture of a frog) -> Students write "jumpy."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"The ___ lives in trees." (Picture of a monkey) -> Students write "monkey."</li>
                    </ul>
                    Teacher guides with sentence frames on the board.
                </li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 3 (Pair Interaction - Peer Check):</b> Students work in pairs to check their answers from Activity 2. They show their completed sentences to their partner. Teacher models: "Is this correct? 'The sloth is slow.'" Partner says "Yes!" or "No, check again." This encourages peer learning and reinforces correct written forms.</li>
            </ul>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15-17 minutes</p>
        </td>
    </tr>

    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students use the language in a realistic scenario: "Discovering Nature’s Wonders," creating a written product.</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Students will create a short "Rainforest List" or "My Favorite Rainforest Animals" list, writing words and simple phrases or sentences. They will respond to a "poll" about their preferences by writing their choice.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b></p>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher provides a worksheet titled "My Rainforest List" with numbered blank lines (1-3) and a small space for drawing, or a simple "Poll" section.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Part A: Make a List.</b> Teacher projects a large image of a rainforest scene (similar to Presentation Stage but with more details like river, leaves, vines). Teacher instructs: "Look at the rainforest picture. What do you see? Write 3 things you see."
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Example words: 'monkey', 'tree', 'river', 'leaf', 'snake', 'toucan'.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students write their list (e.g., "1. monkey 2. tree 3. river"). Teacher reminds them of capital letters and spelling.</li>
                    </ul>
                </li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Part B: Respond to a Question/Poll.</b> Teacher asks a poll question: "Which animal do you like? A slow sloth or a colorful parrot or a noisy monkey?" (Teacher writes these options on the board as cues). Students write their choice below their list (e.g., "I like monkeys." or "Parrot.").</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Optional extension: Students can draw a small picture next to each item on their list.</li>
            </ul>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">8-10 minutes</p>
        </td>
    </tr>

    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy and student self-reflection on their writing output.</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher collects students' "My Rainforest List" worksheets. The teacher uses a simple checklist to observe:
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Did the student make a list of 3 items?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Did the student write a response to the poll/question?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Are the words intelligible (readable)?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Is target vocabulary (e.g., sloth, monkey, parrot, tree, river) used?</li>
                </ul>
            </p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students present their written lists/responses to a classmate. They read their list and their preferred animal. "I see a monkey, a tree, a river. I like monkeys."</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Legibility:</b> Written words/phrases are clear and readable.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of Target Vocabulary:</b> Correctly uses 2-3 words from the rainforest vocabulary (e.g., 'sloth', 'monkey', 'tree', 'colorful', 'slow').</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Completion:</b> Student attempted to make a list and respond to the question.</li>
            </ul>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students orally complete the following sentences before leaving:
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Today I wrote about ______."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"My favorite word to write was ______."</li>
                </ul>
            </p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-6 minutes</p>
        </td>
    </tr>

    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Student and teacher reflection for continuous improvement and lesson sequence connection.</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Reflection Prompt:</b></p>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What new words about the rainforest did you write today?"</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Was it easy or difficult to write your list? Why?"</li>
            </ul>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Reflection:</b></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">What worked well in this lesson? The use of visual aids and guided sentence completion in practice helped scaffold the writing task effectively for 2nd graders. Students were engaged in writing about animals they knew.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for the next lesson? Some students struggled with letter formation for longer words like "rainforest" or "colorful." For future writing tasks, I need to provide more explicit letter practice or tracing activities for new, longer vocabulary. Also, incorporating more opportunities for students to describe animals using "The ___ is ___ and ___" would reinforce adjective+noun grammar.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> The next lesson (Lesson 5: Mediation & Integration) will build on this written output by having students share their lists and descriptions with others, potentially summarizing or explaining their choices, and consolidating all learned vocabulary for a 21st Century Skills Project about protecting the rainforest.</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">2-3 minutes</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>